<speak>
<p>Let u be odd and greater than one. The normalized polynomials c, d, and g each have product with their star equal to u squared. At every subset input used below, each evaluated value has magnitude u. In particular, none is zero.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Let I be the distinct primes dividing u. Choose a primitive p-th root zeta sub p for each prime. For a subset S, multiply its chosen roots to obtain zeta sub S; the empty subset gives one. Delta of f is the alternating product of these evaluations. Even subsets contribute above the fraction bar, and odd subsets below. Node four proves that each of the three Delta values is a root of unity of odd order. We use that theorem as an input.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>Use Delta of c as the reference. Define R as Delta of d divided by Delta of c, and W as Delta of g divided by Delta of c. These are well-defined complex quotients of Delta values. They do not compare individual polynomial coefficients. A common odd exponent will control their orders.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Suppose alpha to the m equals one and beta to the n equals one, with m and n positive and odd. Raise alpha divided by beta to the product m n. Regrouping the powers gives alpha to the m, raised to n, divided by beta to the n, raised to m. Both numerator and denominator become one. The quotient therefore has order dividing the odd number m n. Its order is odd as well. This applies to both R and W. Order one is included.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>For the guiding case u equals three, set omega equal to the exponential of two pi i over three. The two subset roots are one and omega, so Delta of f equals f of one over f of omega. Unpacking R gives d of one over c of one, divided by d of omega over c of omega. W is the same ratio of ratios with g in place of d. The ratio at input one is on top; the ratio at omega is below.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>In this case, Node four restricts the three Delta values to one, omega, and omega squared. Dividing these cubic roots stays in the same three-element set. Thus each comparison has those three candidates. Keep all three available. These points represent complex phases, not residues modulo one plus i.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>If R equals one, the relative ratio d over c is unchanged between the two inputs. The analogous statement holds for W and g over c. This does not say that those two relative ratios equal each other, or that the three evaluated points coincide. We have not yet forced R and W to equal one. The separate sign condition in Node six point three, followed by rigidity in Node six point four, will supply the remaining argument.<break time="1000ms"/></p>
</speak>
